Below is an example program in Java that simulates the rolling of two dice and prints out both the theoretical and empirical probability distributions.

Remember that the sum of two six-sided dice can range from 2 (1+1) to 12 (6+6). The total number of possible outcomes when rolling two dice is 6 * 6 = 36, since each die has 6 faces.

```java
import java.text.DecimalFormat;
import java.util.Random;

public class DiceSimulation {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] rollCounts = simulateDiceRolls(36);
        int[] rollCounts500 = simulateDiceRolls(500);
        int[] rollCounts10000 = simulateDiceRolls(10000);

        // Print the header of the table
        System.out.println("X\tCount\tEm Prob\tP(X)\t\tCount(500)\tEm Prob(500)\tCount(10000)\tEm Prob(10000)");

        // Initialize DecimalFormat for rounding
        DecimalFormat df = new DecimalFormat("#.####");

        // Iterate through possible sum outcomes (2 through 12)
        for (int i = 2; i <= 12; i++) {
            double theoreticalProbability = getTheoreticalProbability(i);
            System.out.printf("%d\t%d\t%s\t%s\t\t%d\t\t%s\t\t%d\t\t%s\n",
                    i,
                    rollCounts[i - 2],
                    df.format(rollCounts[i - 2] / 36.0),
                    df.format(theoreticalProbability),
                    rollCounts500[i - 2],
                    df.format(rollCounts500[i - 2] / 500.0),
                    rollCounts10000[i - 2],
                    df.format(rollCounts10000[i - 2] / 10000.0));
        }
    }

    // Simulates rolling two dice 'numberOfRolls' times and returns the counts for each sum
    public static int[] simulateDiceRolls(int numberOfRolls) {
        Random random = new Random();
        int[] counts = new int[11]; // Arrays to hold the counts for sums 2-12

        // Roll the dice 'numberOfRolls' times
        for (int i = 0; i < numberOfRolls; i++) {
            int die1 = random.nextInt(6) + 1;
            int die2 = random.nextInt(6) + 1;
            int sum = die1 + die2;
            counts[sum - 2]++; // Increment the count for the observed sum
        }
        return counts;
    }

    // Returns the theoretical probability of rolling a given sum with two dice
    public static double getTheoreticalProbability(int sum) {
        int[] occurrences = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1}; // Occurrences of sums 2-12
        return occurrences[sum - 2] / 36.0;
    }
}
```

This Java program constructs a simple simulation for rolling two six-sided dice. It first prints a table header, followed by the sum of the dice (2 to 12), the count of that sum occurring in simulations of different roll counts (36, 500, and 10,000), the empirical probability of each outcome, and the theoretical probability.

Keep in mind that the actual results of your simulation may vary due to the nature of random number generation.

